I wrote `evidence.json` to the Chris Pratt run folder. It passes the repo's evidence check with status **ready**. No other files were touched and nothing was published.

**Type read: 3, medium confidence. Strongest alternative: 7.**

The test I used: when fun and looking successful pull in different directions, which does he protect? A 7 keeps the fun and reframes the loss. Pratt's record keeps the image, by his own account:
- **The sales job (age 19):** when the coupon-sales job collapsed into debt, he "had everyone convinced" he was succeeding (Vanity Fair 2017).
- **The Andy Dwyer period:** he chose the "dumb fat guy" persona because it was "paying better." He later called the fun of that period "That image that I was casting, to convince people that I was okay," and summed up the shift to action roles as "Now I have less fun. I focus more" (GQ 2015).
- **James Gunn, independently:** he saw Pratt "juggle to entertain everybody" and not trust himself "to just be Chris Pratt" (GQ 2015).
- **Recent:** in a March 2026 podcast he calls himself "money motivated my whole life" and "too much of a people pleaser to be a good manager."

The record pushing the other way is recorded honestly. It includes Maui as "a charming time," the hyperactive prankster childhood, "work to live" and his farm, a faith vow he's kept since his son's premature birth in 2012 even though it has cost him with mainstream audiences, and a pattern of other people making his big moves, which points toward 9.

**What happens next:** I flagged both contested typing and sensitive allegations (the Hillsong / anti-LGBTQ church controversy), so the draft will go to the full six-reviewer panel, not the two-reviewer one.

**Traps the draft writer should know about:**
- **Father's MS diagnosis:** Vanity Fair says it came in Pratt's senior year. In the 2026 podcast he says sophomore year. The draft should avoid naming the year.
- **Rob Lowe podcast (Feb 2026):** I re-transcribed the audio. "Would I be funny? Would people like me?" runs straight on from Pratt's own turn, though Whisper can't confirm voices. A nearby line about niceness being "an asset" may be Lowe's, so I excluded it.
- **His son's prematurity:** sources say seven weeks or nine. Use "premature" without a number.
- **Quote checks:** every quoted fragment matches its saved source text (86 checked inside the claims). The podcast quotes agree across two transcription models. Some lines from the other podcasts rely on YouTube auto-captions and still need an audio check before they're quoted.

Two items are still open: Anna Faris's "we both protected that imagery" was read through a GMA report, not the podcast audio, and the 2015 Entertainment Weekly piece behind the wrestling-coach quote blocked automated access, so that quote is attributed as Vanity Fair relaying it.

